Durarara!! AU
Chapter 24 – Part 2 
Curtis left a little early for campus.
The hallway was quiet. The kit stayed behind on the sideboard. Outside, morning Ikebukuro had the thin, honest light of a day that did not know about soft landings or erased desks. He made it three steps from the building entrance before the ground reminded him.
The crack was still there.
Spiderwebbed, ugly, expensive-looking — the same damage Rio had clocked the night she asked about cops and a couple and a man in cuffs. Superhero landing. Bottle to a robber’s head. A moment when the pavement had paid for his durability.
“…Fuck. I gotta look into getting that fixed before my landlord finds out.”
He stood over it a second longer, long black braids shifting against his uniform, then kept walking. There was no white smoke fix for concrete that would not look like a miracle, and miracles invited questions. School was simpler. School had Anri and Rio and Mikado’s late smile and a day that only felt like it knew what he had done last night.
Curtis stepped around the damage and continued on toward Raira, already late in his head for a life that kept leaving marks he was going to have to pay for — in pavement, in fortunes, and in mornings that refused to stay uncomplicated.
Two men slammed into Curtis.
They had already hit a mother and her kid a step earlier — the woman’s shopping bag skidding, the child stumbling with a sharp cry — and they did not slow down until they met something that did not give. Curtis’s hands locked on suit fabric. Long black braids shifted as he turned their momentum into the pavement and put both of them on the ground hard enough to empty their lungs without breaking the street a second time.
The mother pulled her child behind her.
Curtis stood over the men, mismatched eyes flat.
“You just slammed into a mother and her kid before slamming into me.” He took a deep breath. “Both of you better have a good explanation.”
One of the suits started to push up. A woman’s heels clicked in before he found his voice.
“We don’t need to explain anything to a stupid college kid.”
Curtis turned.
Namie Yagiri stood on the morning sidewalk like the city had been built to inconvenience her and she was already billing it for the privilege. Cool eyes. Corporate posture. The kind of presence that did not apologize for men in suits treating pedestrians as obstacles. He filed the face against everything the van gang and the firecracker boy and Kadota’s watch on Yagiri Pharmaceuticals had already put in his head.
His response came out even, not loud.
“…You’re right. You don’t need to explain it to a college kid.” A short pause as he glanced once at the mother and child, then back to Namie. “You need to explain it to the woman and the kid your people just walked over. Or you can keep standing there calling me stupid while I decide how long these two stay on the ground.”
He did not move off the suits.
“Your call, Miss Yagiri.”
Using the name on purpose. Letting her know the “stupid college kid” was not as uninformed as the insult required. Morning traffic flowed around the tableau — mother, child, two professionals on the pavement, a tall foreigner with braids, and Namie Yagiri discovering that Ikebukuro’s pavement still had people on it who did not move when pushed.
Namie’s eyes narrowed a fraction.
“You know my name?”
Curtis did not ease off the men on the ground. Long black braids shifted against his uniform as he answered like he was reading a label instead of starting a war.
“Your stupid face is on some of your pharmaceuticals products. And I’ve seen your face in science and doctor magazines.”
For a second Namie only stared at him.
The insult on stupid face landed; the rest of it landed harder. Not a Dollars rumor. Not an info-broker’s whisper. A tall foreign student who read packaging and journals hard enough to put Yagiri to a sidewalk confrontation. One of the suits started to rise again. She stopped him with two fingers without looking down.
“…Magazines.” The word came out flat, almost amused in a way that had no warmth. “Most people your age can barely read a course syllabus. You’re either lying to sound dangerous, or you’re the kind of nuisance who actually does his homework.”
Her gaze flicked over his braids, his height, the mother and child still half-sheltered behind him, the ease with which two grown men had hit the pavement and stayed there.
“Either way, you’re delaying work that does not concern you.” She tipped her chin toward the men. “Get up. We’re done performing for a college boy who collects faces from pill bottles.”
Then, to Curtis, cooler:
“Remember the name from a magazine if it makes you feel informed. Do not put yourself in front of my staff again. Next time they may not stop at walking through you.”
It was not a full retreat. It was a woman reclaiming the sidewalk without admitting she had just been read by someone who should not have been able to do it before first period.
Curtis did not answer the threat with a speech.
He turned, short and precise, and put both men out before either finished standing — two clean, ugly motions that left suits crumpled on the pavement and the morning air suddenly quieter. The mother pulled her child tighter. A few pedestrians decided they had somewhere else to be.
Then he walked up to Namie.
Long black braids shifted against his uniform. He did not raise his hands. He did not need to. The distance between “college kid” and the bodies on the ground had just collapsed into a single step.
“Still think I’m just a kid?”
Namie did not step back.
That, more than anything, was the tell. Her eyes moved from the unconscious men to Curtis’s face, reassessing with the same cold speed she used on ledgers and liabilities. No scream. No call for more staff in public. Only the sharp recalculation of a woman who had misread the weight of the obstacle in her path.
“…No,” she said at last, voice still even. “I think you’re a problem.”
A short pause. The corner of her mouth did not smile.
“Problems can be managed. Problems can be bought, buried, or assigned to people who clean up messes for a living.” Her gaze flicked once to the mother and child, then back, as if even that had become part of the equation. “You protected a civilian tableau and then escalated in the street. That makes you either principled or unstable. I have not decided which is more inconvenient.”
She adjusted her bag on her shoulder, already turning the sidewalk back into her territory by force of will.
“Enjoy campus, magazine boy. If your name becomes relevant to Yagiri Pharmaceuticals, you will not hear it from me first.”
She walked away without ordering the men to be carried. Someone else would do that. Behind her, Curtis stood over two suits on the ground and a crack in a different pavement still waiting at home, late for school and newly interesting to a woman who did not waste interest on children.
Curtis’s hand closed on Namie’s collar before she finished the turn.
Not a wild grab. A hard, controlled stop that put her back in the scene she had been trying to exit. Long black braids shifted as he leaned in just enough that the morning had to hear him.
“I don’t think so. Go apologize to the woman and child.”
The sidewalk held its breath. The two suits stayed down. The mother stared, child half-hidden in her coat, shopping bag still on the ground where it had skidded.
Namie’s eyes cut to his fist in her collar, then up to his face. For the first time something hotter than calculation showed — not fear, offense — the offense of a person who was not touched without permission in her own city.
“…Take your hand off me.”
Curtis did not.
“Apologize first. Your people hit them. You called it work. Make it right in front of them, or we stay here until someone with a badge asks why two of your suits are napping on the pavement and my hand is in your collar.”
Namie held his gaze a long second, measuring whether the college boy was bluffing, unstable, or exactly as principled and inconvenient as she had named him. Then, without looking away from Curtis, she turned her face slightly toward the mother.
“My staff were careless,” she said, each word clipped and public. “The fault is Yagiri’s. I apologize for the impact and the fright.”
It was not warm. It was not enough for poetry. It was an apology on a sidewalk, extracted under a foreigner’s fist, and the mother gave a tight, startled nod as if she had not expected even that much from a woman in those clothes.
Only then did Curtis open his hand.
Namie stepped back, smoothed her collar once, and looked at him like she was already writing his description into a file that did not exist yet.
“You have made yourself memorable,” she said quietly. “That is rarely a gift.”
She walked away. This time he let her. Behind him, the mother whispered a shaky thank you he did not fully turn to collect, already late for Raira and newly filed in the mind of Namie Yagiri as something worse than a stupid college kid —
a problem that put hands on her and made her say sorry out loud.
Curtis did not let the retreat close the file.
“Now it brings me to my next question.” Long black braids shifted as he tipped his chin toward the street, toward the suits still on the ground, toward her. “What’s the big beautiful boss of Yagiri doing running in the streets?”
Namie stopped.
The beautiful sat in the sentence like a pin — not flattery she could dismiss as student awkwardness, not pure insult either. She turned just enough to look at him over her shoulder, collar still faintly creased from his fist, eyes colder for having had to apologize in public.
“…Boss is a generous word for what you think you’re looking at.” A short, dry pause. “And running implies haste. I was walking. My staff were clearing a path. You turned a commute into theater.”
She faced him fully again, bag strap tight in her hand.
“Yagiri Pharmaceuticals does not live only inside a building, magazine boy. Product, people, and problems move. Sometimes the problems have braids and a hero complex.” Her gaze flicked to the mother and child, then back. “You got your apology. You got your demonstration. If you are fishing for why I am outdoors, the answer is ordinary and will disappoint you: work. Not every woman in a good coat is fleeing a crime scene.”
A thinner smile that was not a smile.
“Enjoy the mystery if you need one for campus. I am late because of you. Next time you put your hands on me, do it with a lawyer present. Or do not do it at all.”
She left for real then, heels cutting away through the morning crowd, two unconscious suits already becoming someone else’s recovery job. Curtis stood on the sidewalk with a new name filed under problem in a Yagiri head, a mother still staring at him, and a school day that had started before first period with Namie Yagiri’s collar in his fist.
Curtis watched her try to leave and did not buy the ordinary work story for a second.
“Hmm. Nope. I don’t buy it.” Long black braids shifted against his uniform as he ticked it off on the open morning air. “First, you and your men are running through the streets. And your brother dropped out on the first day of school. My guess — you’re looking for him.”
Namie stopped as if the sidewalk had dropped a degree.
Seiji’s absence was not public campus gossip in any useful way. A transfer student knowing the dropout and tying it to Yagiri muscle on a weekday morning was not magazine reading. That was a blade sliding under a door she had thought was closed.
When she turned this time, the cold had sharpened into something closer to threat.
“…You are guessing above your grade level.” Each word measured. “My brother’s schooling is a family matter. It is not a puzzle for a foreigner who collects faces off labels and puts his hands on people in the street.”
She took one step back toward him, not away — the move of someone closing a file by force.
“If you have seen Seiji, you will tell me. If you have not, you will stop attaching his name to your little sidewalk performance.” Her eyes flicked over him once more, re-sorting problem into a more dangerous shelf. “Yagiri does not look for lost children in public with two men and a timetable. Remember that, in case your imagination gets busy again between classes.”
It was denial with a pulse under it.
Curtis heard the pulse.
He did not grab her collar again. He only stood there with the mother and child still at the edge of the scene, two suits on the ground, and a new certainty settling in beside the kit and the dullahan and the month:
Namie Yagiri was in the street for a reason that wore her brother’s name, and she had just told him, by how hard she refused it, that he was close enough to hurt.
Curtis’s voice stayed level, but the temperature under it dropped.
“No. Your company simply traffics immigrants with no paper trail.” Long black braids shifted as he let that sit on the morning air between them, heavy with Kaztano and a van and men who put people in ditches. “I might have seen your brother. But what’s it worth to you?”
The sidewalk went very quiet.
Namie looked at him as if the college kid had just put a gun on the table and asked for a price list. For a fraction of a second something raw moved behind her eyes — Seiji, always Seiji — then the corporate ice slammed down harder than before.
“…You are either very brave,” she said softly, “or you have already lived longer than your judgment deserves.”
She did not deny the trafficking charge out loud. That was its own kind of answer. When she spoke again, it was precise enough to cut.
“If you have seen Seiji, you will not auction him on a street corner like a stolen pallet. You will give me a location, a time, and every detail you claim to have. In return, you will not be added to a list of obstacles my family removes when they become loud.” A short pause. “If you are bluffing — if might have seen is a foreigner’s way of fishing — then you have just accused Yagiri Pharmaceuticals of trafficking in public while standing over two of my men. That is not leverage. That is a request to be destroyed.”
Her gaze flicked to the mother and child, then back, colder for having had to care about witnesses.
“Name your proof or walk to school. There is no third option where you get paid in mystery and good conscience.”
Curtis held her eyes, the cartel years and the van-gang rescue and a dullahan’s erased desk all stacked behind a single dangerous bluff, and smiled without warmth.
The morning had stopped being about a collar and an apology.
It had become a negotiation with a woman who would burn a city block for her brother — and had not denied what her company did to people with no paper trail.
Curtis shook his head once, long black braids shifting against his uniform.
“I don’t want money.” The words came out flat, almost bored with the offer she had not quite made. “Your lab coat and education along with your social status makes you stupid.”
For a heartbeat Namie only stared.
Then the cold cracked into something sharper — not volume, precision. A woman who had built herself on credentials being told those credentials were the blindfold.
“…Say that again.”
Curtis did not bother.
“You heard me. Degrees and a name on a bottle and a coat that makes people step aside — and you still walk your muscle through a mother and a kid like the sidewalk belongs to the letterhead. That’s not intelligence. That’s insulation. You think the paper and the title mean the street has to move. It doesn’t. People do. And when they don’t, you call it a problem instead of looking at your own feet.”
Namie’s jaw tightened. The mother and child had gone still at the edge of the scene, sensing the air change again. The two suits remained a quiet argument on the pavement.
“You have a talented mouth for someone who has not yet been made to regret it,” she said, voice low. “I am not stupid. I am efficient. Efficiency looks cruel to people who still believe the world owes them soft landings and apologies on demand.” Her eyes cut over his braids, his height, the ease of the violence he had already spent. “You refuse money. You lecture status. You dangle my brother like a rumor. So tell me, magazine boy — if not cash and not a clean conscience, what exactly do you think you are buying with this performance?”
It was not a retreat.
It was a woman re-sorting him from problem into unknown variable that talks like it has already survived worse than Yagiri, and hating that the sort was taking longer than it should on a public street before first period.
Curtis did not blink.
“I have seen your brother.” Long black braids shifted against his uniform as he let the upgrade from might land. “But I require a return. What can you offer? Money is pointless.”
Namie’s posture went very still.
Confirmation was different from a fishhook. Have seen put Seiji in a real place at a real time in a foreigner’s memory, and she felt the pull of it in spite of every instinct that said do not bargain with street theater.
“…You have seen him.” Not a question. A restatement, tasting for a lie. “Where. When. In what condition.”
Curtis only waited.
She understood the shape of the trade a second later — he would not dump the detail into the open air for free, and cash had already been refused. Her mouth tightened. Efficiency, again. Find the currency the obstacle actually spent.
“Information,” she said at last. “You are a student who reads journals and pharmaceutical labels and somehow knows enough about immigrant paper trails to throw the word trafficking on a sidewalk. That is either dangerous knowledge or a dangerous mouth. I can offer clarity in return for clarity. You tell me when and where you saw Seiji. I tell you whether the thing you think you know about Yagiri’s… logistics… is as simple as you made it sound.”
A thinner pause.
“Or protection. Temporary. Quiet. If you have made enemies by putting your hands on my staff and my collar, I can make those enemies less interested. Still not money. Still a return.”
Her eyes did not soften.
“Those are the offers. Location and condition of my brother first. If you are inventing him to feel tall, this conversation ends with you learning how long a Yagiri grudge lasts. If you are not — speak.”
Morning traffic flowed around them. The mother had finally edged away with her child. The suits still had not moved. Curtis stood in the middle of a negotiation that had started with a slammed shoulder and was now priced in a brother’s shadow, and Namie Yagiri was waiting to see whether the college kid’s next sentence was leverage or a suicide note.
Curtis looked past Namie at the staff who still had not found their feet, then back to her face.
“I’m not afraid of you peons.” Long black braids shifted against his uniform, mismatched eyes flat. “I have faced worse nightmares. So drop your threats on my life. Or do you want to be planted into the ground like your men.”
He did not raise his voice. He did not need volume when the two suits were already a demonstration.
“Look around you, Namie. No one is going to stop me and no one can. Choose your next words very, very carefully.”
The morning seemed to thin.
Pedestrians had already made themselves scarce. The mother and child were gone. Whatever cameras the street owned were about to become a problem for someone else. Namie held his gaze and, for the first time since the collar, did not reach for a threat she could not cash on the pavement in front of her.
When she spoke, the corporate ice was still there — but the death-promise was gone from it.
“…You have made your point.” Each word chosen like a step on thin glass. “No more threats. Not from me. Not on this street.”
A short breath through her nose.
“You want a return that is not money. Fine. I will give you a direct line — my personal number — and a single honest answer to one question about Yagiri’s operations that does not endanger my brother. In exchange you give me when and where you saw Seiji, and whether he was alone. That is the trade. No lists. No ‘obstacles removed.’ No planting anyone else in the ground.”
Her eyes flicked once to the men, then back.
“You are not a college kid. I was wrong about that. Do not make me wrong about the rest of my patience. Speak the sighting, or walk away from a deal that will not be offered twice.”
It was as close to careful as Namie Yagiri got in public — a woman who had just been told she could be put in the street like her staff, choosing words that kept her standing.
Curtis shook his head once, almost patient.
“The info I have is going to cost you more than that, Namie.” Long black braids shifted against his uniform. “I can even tell you when to find him. But it’s going to cost more than what you’re offering. Stop lowballing me. If Seiji means this much to you, then you can offer more than that.”
Namie’s mouth went thin.
When to find him changed the temperature again. Not a past sighting alone — a window. A schedule. Something a desperate sister could put men on. She looked at the suits on the ground, at the empty stretch of sidewalk that had decided not to intervene, at the tall foreigner who kept refusing the cheap currencies and still had not blinked.
“…You are enjoying this,” she said quietly.
“I’m pricing it,” Curtis answered. “Different thing.”
She was silent long enough that a bus sighed past at the corner. When she spoke again, the offer had weight.
“Fine. Above a number and a single operational answer.” She counted it off without theater. “One: my personal line, as before, for as long as the information remains accurate. Two: a full, private accounting of the immigrant ‘logistics’ you threw in my face — what is real, what is rumor, and which names on the street are actually ours. Three: if your information puts me in the same room as Seiji within seventy-two hours, I will owe you a favor that is not money and not a threat. One favor. Scoped by you. Within reason that does not require me to burn the company to ash.”
Her eyes locked on his.
“That is no longer a lowball. That is me putting my brother’s recovery on a foreigner’s word in public. You give me the when — and the where it attaches to — or you admit you stretched a first-day campus glimpse into a ransom and we discover which of us was bluffing.”
She did not look at the men this time.
She looked only at Curtis, waiting to see whether Chief of the North and magazine boy and soft-landing complication was about to sell a brother by the clock, or whether the next sentence would be the one that finally made Namie Yagiri walk away empty.
Curtis laughed.
It came out sharp and sudden, long black braids shifting as he tipped his head back like the whole sidewalk trade had just told a joke only he could hear.
“Hahahahah. Pointless.” He looked at her again, mismatched eyes bright with something that was not kindness. “I already know why you traffic. I figured it out a while ago. No — I guess this conversation is becoming pointless. You have nothing I want.”
The offer — line, accounting, favor, seventy-two hours — hung in the air between them and died.
Namie stared.
For a woman who lived on leverage, being told her entire spread was worthless was worse than the collar. Worse than the suits on the ground. The laugh made it personal.
“…You—” She stopped, recalibrated, and when she spoke again the ice had gone brittle. “You staged all of this. The mother. The men. The lecture about status. The I have seen your brother. And at the end of it you claim you already hold the only card that matters and want nothing in return.”
A short, dangerous breath.
“That is not how people bargain, magazine boy. That is how people announce they are already inside the house.” Her voice dropped. “If you know why Yagiri moves the people it moves, then you know enough to be a liability. If you have seen Seiji and still walk away from every return I put on the table, then either you are protecting him… or you are waiting for a price I have not named yet.”
She stepped in half a pace, close enough that the morning had to notice.
“Which is it. Because I do not believe in pointless conversations. I believe in people who smile when they have already decided how this ends.”
Curtis only watched her, the laugh gone, the cartel years and a dullahan’s head and a brother in a photo on a sideboard stacked behind a refusal that had just made Namie Yagiri more afraid of him than when his hand was on her collar.
He had nothing he wanted from her.
That, to a woman like Namie, was the most expensive sentence on the street.
Curtis’s voice went quiet and final.
“You owe me. Simple.” Long black braids shifted against his uniform. “I make a call for what I want and when I want it. Sound good? It could be anything. You double-cross me at any point — believe me, you will never see Seiji again.”
The street seemed to narrow around the sentence.
Namie did not move. For a long second the only sound was a distant crossing signal and the soft, useless shift of one suit’s shoe on the pavement. When she answered, it was without theatrics, without the magazine-boy insults, without any pretense that she still controlled the price.
“…An open call.” She tasted the shape of it. “No scope. No timeline. No guarantee it stays inside the law or inside the company.”
“That’s the deal,” Curtis said. “You want your brother. I keep what I know until I collect. You stay honest with the debt. You go sideways — Seiji becomes a ghost you don’t get to chase.”
Namie’s eyes held his. Somewhere behind the ice, Seiji’s name did the math she would not say out loud: a head, a lab, a life she had already bent the world to keep.
“I accept.”
Two words. No handshake. No phone number offered yet — that would come when he decided the call was due. She stepped back, smoothed the collar he had already wrinkled once this morning, and looked at him as if she were photographing a new kind of enemy.
“When you call,” she said, low, “you had better still be worth the brother you are holding hostage with a smile. And if you are lying about having seen him at all…”
She did not finish the threat. He had already taught her how those landed.
“I will be in touch, Namie.” Curtis tipped his chin toward the men on the ground. “Get your peons off my sidewalk. I have class.”
He turned toward Raira without waiting for her to move first.
Behind him, Namie Yagiri stood over two unconscious staff and an open-ended debt owed to a foreigner with braids, already rewriting every file she had on what a “stupid college kid” could cost a company that trafficked in the dark and loved one boy enough to say I accept on a public street.
Curtis paused before the last step toward campus, as if the debt deserved a first installment that was not free and not complete.
“You won’t find him during the day.” Long black braids shifted against his uniform. “You two seem to miss each other. Are you busy in the evening?”
Namie’s nod was automatic, already living in labs and ledgers after dark.
“That tracks.” Curtis’s voice stayed even. “I’ve seen him with some girl with a scar on her neck in the evening.”
The color did not leave Namie’s face so much as lock.
A scar on the neck. Evening. Seiji not alone. The shape of it slammed into every private file she kept on the head, the dullahan, the thing her family had built a second life around. For a moment the street, the suits, the open call — all of it narrowed to a single foreign sentence.
“…A girl,” she repeated, careful. “Scar on the neck. You’re certain.”
“I’m not in the business of being uncertain when I put a brother’s name on a debt,” Curtis said. “Evening. With her. You’re looking in the wrong hours and maybe at the wrong kind of company.”
Namie’s hands had gone still at her sides. When she spoke again the corporate gloss was gone; what remained was a sister who had just been handed a direction she could not ignore.
“Describe the girl.”
Curtis only smiled, thin and final.
“That’s enough for a first payment. You have the when-shape. You have the company-shape. The rest stays behind the call I get to make.” He tipped his chin toward Raira. “Stay busy in the evenings if you want. Or don’t. That’s your problem now.”
He walked on.
Namie did not follow. She stood over her men with a new image burning behind her eyes — Seiji, after dark, beside a girl whose neck carried a scar — and understood, with a cold that had nothing to do with the morning air, that the college boy with braids had not only priced a brother.
He had pointed her at a shadow she already hunted, and then walked to class like the most dangerous thing on the street was already late for first period.
Namie’s fists clenched at her sides.
The foreigner was already disappearing into the morning crowd — long black braids, Raira uniform, the easy gait of someone who had just put an open debt on a Yagiri and walked toward first period like the street owed him the sidewalk. Behind her, two men in suits were starting to remember how knees worked. In front of her, the space where he had stood still felt occupied.
Smart like Izaya.
The comparisons arrived uninvited and precise. The way he had refused money, refused the lowball, refused even the satisfaction of a clean sale — then left her with a partial truth timed to hurt. That was Orihara’s breed of mind: information as a leash, generosity as a threat.
Tough like Shizuo.
Not the screaming kind. The other kind. The kind that put bodies on the pavement without raising its voice and promised to plant a Yagiri in the ground like it was a weather report. He had not flinched when she reached for death as currency. He had told her to choose her next words carefully and meant it.
Intelligent like her.
That one stung. The trafficking motive already mapped. The evening window. The girl with the scar on her neck — a detail that could only come from eyes that paid attention in the dark. He had read product labels and journals and immigrant paper trails and her brother’s absence from a classroom, then built a negotiation she could not exit without saying I accept.
Namie forced her hands open.
“Get up,” she told the men, voice flat. “Find a car. We are not discussing this on a public street.”
They scrambled. She did not watch them. She watched the direction Curtis Nightstar had gone, already filing a new category that did not exist on any Yagiri chart:
Not a college kid.
Not only a problem.
A hybrid of the three people in Ikebukuro she least wanted standing on the same sidewalk as her brother’s name —
and he had just walked to school holding the hours she had been missing Seiji in, and the scarred throat that sat at the center of everything her family had ever done in the dark.
The car doors shut on the morning like a lid.
One of the men — the one with the newer bruise along his jaw — twisted in the passenger seat, voice low and practical.
“We can eliminate the boy. Quiet. Before he talks to anyone else. He put hands on you. He knows things he should not know. It solves itself.”
Namie did not look up from her phone.
“You will not touch him.”
The second man glanced at the rearview. “Namie-san—”
“You will not touch him,” she repeated, each word cut clean, “and you will not go near him. Not on campus. Not on the street. Not in a hallway with soft lighting and a story about self-defense. If either of you so much as tests his distance, you answer to me before you answer to a hospital.”
Silence held for a stretch of traffic.
The first man shifted, still not understanding the shape of the order. “He’s a student. We can make him disappear.”
Namie’s eyes lifted. Whatever had clenched in her fists on the sidewalk was ice again, but it was aimed inward at her own staff now.
“He put both of you on the ground without drawing a crowd or a blade. He refused money. He priced my brother in an open call and walked away late for class. You do not eliminate that. You catalog it. You stay away from it until I say otherwise.” A short pause. “He is useful. He is dangerous. He is currently the only person who has given me an evening window and a girl with a scar on her neck. You touch him, that window closes. Permanently.”
She looked back at the phone, already typing a private note that did not exist on any company server:
Curtis Nightstar. Do not approach. Debt active. Seiji — evenings. Female, neck scar.
“Drive,” she said. “If I want a body, I will not ask the two people he already folded into the pavement.”
The car moved.
Behind them, Raira’s morning continued without knowing that Namie Yagiri had just put a foreign student with braids on a protected list for the worst possible reason —
because he might be the only path she still had to her brother, and because the men in the car still thought eliminate was a word that applied to him.